    why: "Writing and debugging drivers is the core of BSP and embedded Linux roles, and 'what is a platform driver?' is asked often.",
    problem: "Starting with complex PCI or network drivers overwhelms; the kernel's rules (contexts, locking, user-copy, managed resources) must be learned on simple drivers first.",
    eli12: "A kernel driver is a specialist who works inside the operating system's office. It must follow strict office rules: never take too long, never touch someone else's desk without permission, and clean up after itself.",
    tech: "Out-of-tree modules (Kbuild, obj-m), module_init/module_exit, logging with dev_info; character devices (misc_register), file_operations (open, read, write, poll, unlocked_ioctl, mmap), copy_to_user/copy_from_user; the device model: buses, devices, drivers; platform drivers matched through of_match_table with probe/remove; I2C and SPI client drivers; managed resources (devm_*); sysfs attributes; interrupts with devm_request_threaded_irq, top and bottom halves, workqueues, wait queues; locking: spinlocks in atomic/interrupt context, mutexes in process context; DMA API awareness. Kernel coding style and checkpatch. Practise in QEMU first.",
    inChip: "Silicon vendors write and upstream drivers for their SoCs; your own FPGA or ASIC peripheral needs one too (the crossover project).",
    breaks: "Sleeping in atomic context, missing error unwinding, races between the IRQ handler and read(), use-after-free on remove.",
